1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,900
In February 2022, Russia launched
a full-scale invasion of Ukraine.

2
00:00:05,720 --> 00:00:09,840
One of the reasons Moscow gave
was NATO creeping towards its borders.

3
00:00:10,658 --> 00:00:13,458
Just over a year later,
Finland joined NATO.

4
00:00:14,338 --> 00:00:18,438
Finland's border with Russia is more
than thirteen hundred kilometres long.

5
00:00:18,978 --> 00:00:23,318
Overnight, the land border
between Russia and NATO roughly doubled.

6
00:00:23,757 --> 00:00:25,357
Then Sweden joined too.

7
00:00:25,437 --> 00:00:29,197
A country that had not fought a war
since 1814.

8
00:00:29,935 --> 00:00:32,935
A war partly justified by NATO's expansion

9
00:00:33,155 --> 00:00:36,915
left Russia with far more NATO
on its border than before.

10
00:00:38,247 --> 00:00:40,707
And that is not the only strange thing.

11
00:00:41,387 --> 00:00:43,887
Russia is the largest country on Earth.

12
00:00:44,467 --> 00:00:47,247
It covers more than a tenth
of all the land on the planet.

13
00:00:48,033 --> 00:00:50,213
It has oil, gas, forests, farmland,

14
00:00:50,443 --> 00:00:53,733
and some of the largest mineral reserves
in the world.

15
00:00:54,073 --> 00:00:59,473
Yet measured by nominal GDP, its economy
is roughly the size of Italy's.

16
00:00:59,673 --> 00:01:02,933
And Russia has more than twice
as many people.

17
00:01:03,515 --> 00:01:05,995
So why does a country with so much
keep choosing confrontation

18
00:01:06,225 --> 00:01:08,535
with neighbours it could trade with?

19
00:01:08,755 --> 00:01:11,835
Even when the price
is paid by Russia itself?

20
00:01:13,635 --> 00:01:17,435
The easy answer is one man.
Vladimir Putin.

21
00:01:18,085 --> 00:01:20,925
But Poland and the Baltic states
were asking to join NATO

22
00:01:21,155 --> 00:01:23,225
long before he came to power.

23
00:01:23,855 --> 00:01:26,215
The pattern is older
than any one president.

24
00:01:26,635 --> 00:01:30,495
And to see who it really serves,
we have to start much earlier.

25
00:01:31,164 --> 00:01:34,644
By the way, if you like videos
like this, consider subscribing.

26
00:01:35,264 --> 00:01:36,884
It really helps the channel.

27
00:01:39,254 --> 00:01:42,054
For five centuries, Moscow grew outwards.

28
00:01:42,774 --> 00:01:46,394
A small principality became an empire
that reached the Pacific.

29
00:01:47,117 --> 00:01:49,337
Much of that growth came by conquest.

30
00:01:50,097 --> 00:01:51,577
And to hold it all together,

31
00:01:51,657 --> 00:01:54,977
power was gathered at the centre,
in the hands of the tsar.

32
00:01:55,578 --> 00:01:56,918
Geography mattered.

33
00:01:57,558 --> 00:02:00,918
Much of western Russia
is a wide, open plain.

34
00:02:01,078 --> 00:02:05,038
No mountains, no sea
between Moscow and an invading army.

35
00:02:05,298 --> 00:02:06,558
And armies came.

36
00:02:07,418 --> 00:02:10,278
Polish troops occupied Moscow in 1610.

37
00:02:10,918 --> 00:02:13,258
Napoleon reached it in 1812.

38
00:02:14,078 --> 00:02:16,018
Hitler's armies came close in 1941.

39
00:02:17,219 --> 00:02:21,679
The Second World War cost the Soviet Union
around twenty-seven million lives.

40
00:02:22,349 --> 00:02:23,749
That fear is real.

41
00:02:23,829 --> 00:02:26,349
And it deserves to be taken seriously.

42
00:02:27,111 --> 00:02:30,151
But fear does not decide
what a country does with it.

43
00:02:30,731 --> 00:02:34,371
You can defend your borders without
claiming the right to run your neighbours.

44
00:02:37,081 --> 00:02:39,221
Here is where the trouble starts.

45
00:02:39,821 --> 00:02:43,721
If you only feel safe when your neighbours
are weak, you will try to keep them weak.

46
00:02:44,390 --> 00:02:46,470
More influence. More control.

47
00:02:47,070 --> 00:02:49,930
A buffer of countries
that cannot choose against you.

48
00:02:50,239 --> 00:02:52,279
But your neighbours have fears too.

49
00:02:52,759 --> 00:02:54,619
And now you are the thing they fear.

50
00:02:55,008 --> 00:02:58,848
So they look for protection.
Alliances. Armies.

51
00:02:58,928 --> 00:03:00,448
Partners far away.

52
00:03:00,781 --> 00:03:04,781
And you see their defence as proof
that the world is closing in on you.

53
00:03:05,315 --> 00:03:08,995
Each side's search for safety
makes the other side less safe.

54
00:03:09,715 --> 00:03:12,735
Political scientists call it
the security dilemma.

55
00:03:14,055 --> 00:03:16,475
Finland shows how fast it can turn.

56
00:03:17,235 --> 00:03:21,055
For decades, only about one Finn in four
wanted to join NATO.

57
00:03:21,855 --> 00:03:25,235
Within three months of the invasion,
three in four did.

58
00:03:25,855 --> 00:03:28,295
Nobody had to talk Finland into it.

59
00:03:28,895 --> 00:03:30,675
Moscow did that by itself.

60
00:03:33,285 --> 00:03:37,045
In the twentieth century,
the empire became the Soviet Union.

61
00:03:38,025 --> 00:03:42,525
A nuclear superpower, with world-class
science, and the first human in orbit.

62
00:03:43,039 --> 00:03:44,799
But it was run the same way.

63
00:03:45,359 --> 00:03:48,019
Power at the centre,
and no room for opposition.

64
00:03:48,854 --> 00:03:51,594
A system that could mobilise
almost anything...

65
00:03:51,834 --> 00:03:54,154
except the truth about its own mistakes.

66
00:03:55,630 --> 00:03:58,370
In 1991, it fell apart.

67
00:03:59,010 --> 00:04:01,550
Russia was left with about
three quarters of the territory,

68
00:04:01,780 --> 00:04:03,930
and only half of the people.

69
00:04:04,560 --> 00:04:07,940
Fourteen other republics
became independent countries.

70
00:04:08,340 --> 00:04:11,660
Most had no wish
to be ruled from Moscow again.

71
00:04:12,274 --> 00:04:14,454
The decade that followed was brutal.

72
00:04:15,354 --> 00:04:21,672
Between 1991 and 1998, Russia's economy
shrank by around forty per cent.

73
00:04:21,972 --> 00:04:24,892
Hyperinflation wiped out
the savings of millions.

74
00:04:25,332 --> 00:04:28,952
Life expectancy for Russian men
fell below sixty.

75
00:04:29,729 --> 00:04:31,549
Meanwhile, a handful of insiders

76
00:04:31,779 --> 00:04:35,829
picked up some of the biggest companies
for a fraction of their value.

77
00:04:36,417 --> 00:04:37,577
For millions of people,

78
00:04:37,657 --> 00:04:41,297
freedom arrived together with chaos,
crime, and humiliation.

79
00:04:41,966 --> 00:04:46,286
And when freedom feels like chaos, a
promise of order becomes very attractive.

80
00:04:46,366 --> 00:04:49,006
Even if it costs some freedom.

81
00:04:51,426 --> 00:04:55,826
On the last day of 1999,
Vladimir Putin became acting president.

82
00:04:56,566 --> 00:04:58,526
He promised exactly that.

83
00:04:58,986 --> 00:05:00,726
Order, and a strong state.

84
00:05:01,082 --> 00:05:03,202
And for a while, it seemed to work.

85
00:05:04,022 --> 00:05:07,162
Oil rose from under twenty dollars
a barrel to over a hundred.

86
00:05:07,782 --> 00:05:10,642
Wages and pensions grew year after year.

87
00:05:11,187 --> 00:05:16,067
The state took back control of the biggest
oil and gas companies, and of television.

88
00:05:17,047 --> 00:05:21,507
And many Russians accepted less political
competition in exchange for stability.

89
00:05:22,179 --> 00:05:24,279
But there is a trap in that bargain.

90
00:05:25,099 --> 00:05:27,619
Institutions too weak to challenge
a popular leader today

91
00:05:27,849 --> 00:05:30,599
are too weak to stop
a destructive one tomorrow.

92
00:05:33,299 --> 00:05:35,179
There was also a wound to heal.

93
00:05:36,199 --> 00:05:39,879
In 2005, Putin called the collapse
of the Soviet Union

94
00:05:40,109 --> 00:05:44,219
the greatest geopolitical catastrophe
of the twentieth century.

95
00:05:44,703 --> 00:05:48,083
In that view, the neighbours
are not simply independent countries.

96
00:05:48,283 --> 00:05:50,963
They are a lost sphere of influence.

97
00:05:51,283 --> 00:05:54,703
And their choices become questions
of Russian prestige.

98
00:05:55,577 --> 00:05:57,577
Georgia, 2008.

99
00:05:57,757 --> 00:05:59,437
Crimea, 2014.

100
00:06:00,197 --> 00:06:02,617
In 2021, Putin published an essay

101
00:06:02,847 --> 00:06:06,137
arguing that Russians and Ukrainians
are one people.

102
00:06:06,577 --> 00:06:09,737
Months later,
the tanks crossed the border.

103
00:06:10,385 --> 00:06:12,525
But Ukraine is not a lost province.

104
00:06:13,245 --> 00:06:16,545
It is a country,
with its own history and its own choices.

105
00:06:17,425 --> 00:06:20,465
Living next door
does not give anyone the right to rule.

106
00:06:23,125 --> 00:06:26,065
So far, this sounds like a story
about ideas.

107
00:06:26,945 --> 00:06:31,445
But there is a second mechanism,
and it is about how decisions get made.

108
00:06:32,077 --> 00:06:35,457
Three days before the invasion,
Russian television broadcast

109
00:06:35,687 --> 00:06:37,917
a meeting of the Security Council.

110
00:06:38,658 --> 00:06:42,098
One by one, the top officials
stood up and backed the decision

111
00:06:42,328 --> 00:06:45,758
to recognise two breakaway regions
in eastern Ukraine.

112
00:06:46,198 --> 00:06:47,538
When the foreign intelligence chief

113
00:06:47,768 --> 00:06:50,758
stumbled over his words,
Putin cut him off.

114
00:06:51,178 --> 00:06:53,418
Speak plainly, he told him.

115
00:06:53,933 --> 00:06:56,413
It was a glimpse of a system
where the safest thing

116
00:06:56,643 --> 00:06:59,333
to tell the leader
is what he already believes.

117
00:07:00,016 --> 00:07:03,796
When bad news is dangerous,
bad news stops travelling upwards.

118
00:07:04,516 --> 00:07:06,916
Loyalty becomes worth more than judgement.

119
00:07:07,751 --> 00:07:10,211
Russia still has brilliant experts.

120
00:07:11,091 --> 00:07:12,571
But expertise is not much use

121
00:07:12,801 --> 00:07:16,031
when an inconvenient conclusion
can cost you your career.

122
00:07:16,660 --> 00:07:18,460
A war that Moscow reportedly expected

123
00:07:18,690 --> 00:07:22,120
to take Kyiv within days
is now in its fifth year.

124
00:07:25,050 --> 00:07:26,950
And there is a third mechanism.

125
00:07:27,750 --> 00:07:31,090
The one that explains
why confrontation keeps coming back.

126
00:07:31,740 --> 00:07:36,680
A government in conflict with the outside
world can treat its critics as traitors.

127
00:07:37,119 --> 00:07:41,459
Since 2012, Russian law has branded
more than a thousand journalists,

128
00:07:41,659 --> 00:07:45,439
academics, activists, and organisations
as foreign agents.

129
00:07:46,196 --> 00:07:49,436
Economic trouble can be blamed
on hostile foreign powers.

130
00:07:49,516 --> 00:07:54,176
And loyalty to the government
can be sold as loyalty to the nation.

131
00:07:54,634 --> 00:07:58,014
That does not mean every conflict
is staged as a distraction.

132
00:07:58,914 --> 00:08:00,314
The point is simpler.

133
00:08:00,983 --> 00:08:05,283
Once a system depends on an enemy,
peace becomes politically awkward.

134
00:08:06,500 --> 00:08:09,620
Which brings us back to the question
of who this pattern serves.

135
00:08:09,700 --> 00:08:11,940
Not what is good for Russia.

136
00:08:12,400 --> 00:08:14,760
What is good for the people who run it.

137
00:08:15,447 --> 00:08:20,187
A country benefits from trade, investment,
and neighbours who are not afraid of it.

138
00:08:20,712 --> 00:08:24,192
A ruling circle with few limits
on its power needs something else.

139
00:08:24,792 --> 00:08:27,152
Control. Loyalty.

140
00:08:27,512 --> 00:08:29,412
And no one able to replace it.

141
00:08:29,965 --> 00:08:32,105
Oil and gas make that easier.

142
00:08:32,805 --> 00:08:36,185
In some years, they brought in
about half of all federal revenue.

143
00:08:36,911 --> 00:08:40,251
A state that lives off exports
needs its taxpayers less.

144
00:08:40,951 --> 00:08:43,011
What it needs is control of the pipes.

145
00:08:43,793 --> 00:08:46,813
And the bill for confrontation
is paid by the country,

146
00:08:46,893 --> 00:08:48,933
not by the people who chose it.

147
00:08:50,057 --> 00:08:52,077
Before the invasion, Russia supplied

148
00:08:52,307 --> 00:08:56,297
about forty-five per cent of the gas
the European Union imported.

149
00:08:56,897 --> 00:08:59,997
By 2025, it was about thirteen.

150
00:09:00,779 --> 00:09:05,519
Hundreds of thousands of Russians left the
country, many of them young and skilled.

151
00:09:06,072 --> 00:09:09,352
And Russia now leans heavily
on a single large partner.

152
00:09:09,552 --> 00:09:10,752
China.

153
00:09:11,418 --> 00:09:13,238
Russia sells it mostly oil and gas.

154
00:09:13,468 --> 00:09:17,438
China sells back cars, machines,
and electronics.

155
00:09:18,279 --> 00:09:20,519
China buys a lot of Russian oil.

156
00:09:20,879 --> 00:09:22,479
But it has other suppliers.

157
00:09:23,079 --> 00:09:26,239
Russia, cut off from Europe,
has far fewer buyers.

158
00:09:27,019 --> 00:09:31,419
A war meant to prove Russia's independence
has left it more dependent than before.

159
00:09:34,559 --> 00:09:36,499
So is this simply Russia's fate?

160
00:09:37,159 --> 00:09:41,359
The old empire, the open plain,
a national character that cannot change?

161
00:09:42,145 --> 00:09:45,105
Not really. Geography explains the fear.

162
00:09:45,525 --> 00:09:47,265
History explains the habits.

163
00:09:47,765 --> 00:09:49,385
Neither one makes the choices.

164
00:09:51,655 --> 00:09:55,395
The pattern keeps returning
because it serves a particular system.

165
00:09:56,155 --> 00:09:59,895
One that needs an enemy abroad
to justify its power at home.

166
00:10:00,457 --> 00:10:02,577
And that raises a harder question.

167
00:10:03,477 --> 00:10:06,977
If the pattern comes from the system,
what would it take to change it?

168
00:10:07,457 --> 00:10:09,037
And how long would that take?

169
00:10:09,887 --> 00:10:11,867
And that is where we go next.

170
00:10:11,947 --> 00:10:15,247
Because replacing one leader
may be the easy part.

171
00:10:15,327 --> 00:10:18,677
Changing the system around him
is another story.
